From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: realign a subtitle file on the video's speech

A subtitle file beside the video is realigned on the video's own
subtitle tracks. Most videos a file is found beside have none: a web
release, a rip of the main film only. For those, the speech is the only
reference there is.

Where someone speaks is heard with Silero's voice activity model (v5, MIT
licence): a short-time spectrum, four convolutions and a recurrent cell,
run here without a runtime - three hundred thousand weights, a chance of
speech every 32 ms. The weights come in an add-on,
script.module.subtitlespeech.allolive, asked for the first time they are
needed; until it is installed nothing is heard and nothing changes.

The subtitle shows a line where someone speaks and nothing in the
silences between, so each change to its times - an offset, under each
frame-rate ratio a release can be off by - is scored by how much more
speech than usual its lines then cover. The best one is taken only when
it scores 1.2 times any other and stands five spreads above the scores
of all offsets; a change of less than half a second is no change, as
lines show a little before their speech. A line that translates writing
on screen, which no one speaks, moves with the rest.

A subtitle made for another cut of the film - an extended edition, a
version with a scene trimmed - is right in places and a few seconds or
minutes off in others. When runs of lines clearly ask for other offsets
than the one change, the lines are laid in parts instead: each run of at
least eight lines at the offset its speech asks for, a switch between
two costing as much as four seconds of agreement, under the best ratio
or under none when that is a near twin of it, as a few steps look like a
slow drift. The parts are taken only when they agree better than any one
offset, and a stretch of a part that clearly agrees with another offset
is left where it was. Each line moves by the part it starts in. One
change that a run of lines clearly contradicts is not taken.

The sound is read a minute at a time across the film, in the background,
from the stream playback starts with: its centre channel when the mix
has one, where the dialogue is. A verdict is asked for after eight
minutes, then sixteen, thirty-two, and so on until the film is heard
whole, and the reading stops at the first clear single change - a file
found right stops it too. A subtitle laid in parts, or not yet clear,
is then read ahead of playback, five minutes at a time, and laid again
every two minutes; lines still to come move when that changes them by
half a second or more. A fit is withdrawn only after two unclear
verdicts in a row. The video is read no faster than 25 MB/s per stretch
and, before playback is followed, no more than 12 GB of it, without the
file cache; closing the subtitle stops it. A DTS-HD stream is decoded to
its core only.

On 46 films of the library, the embedded text subtitle of each was laid
on its own speech as it was, moved by known offsets, and cut to match a
longer or shorter edit of the film - one to three stretches of a few
seconds to four minutes added or removed. Heard whole: every subtitle
as it was was left alone; 79% of the lines of moved subtitles were put
back, none anywhere else; of 370,000 lines under a different cut, 82%
were put right against 56% with one change, and 1.3% moved wrong against
5.2%. Under a different cut and a PAL speed-up together, parts rarely
hold: 19% were put right, 0.5% moved wrong, against 36% and 15% with one
change. No other film's subtitle was accepted. Forced-only subtitles -
the real forced tracks of four films, and 192 made of a few scenes and
signs - were left alone or put right in all 200 trials. Three films the
library has in two cuts were laid each on the other's speech: one
release's lines went from 111 to 224 of 285 right, another's from 32 to
292 of 782 with 9 moved wrong; the third, re-edited all through, was
left alone. Steps of two seconds or less cannot be told apart, and a
stretch re-edited every few lines takes the offset of the part around
it. On the box, a minute of sound is decoded and heard in about three
seconds of one core.

The help of "Realign with the video" says so.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
